-# Inquisitio 0.4.1 Release Notes
+# Inquisitio 0.4.2 extraction correction
 
-## Evidence-to-answer engine
+The extraction and insertion path now retains nearby labels and values, associates named patient/operator records with the responding party, and populates supported selected-form fields with validated page citations. The application has complete target lists for Forms 202 and 207, explicit conflict handling, local OCR and interactive-field reading. The stale automatic-insertion notice is corrected; OCR citations are labeled in the existing inspector and exports.
 
-- Added `inquisitio/engine.py` with case context, per-item requirements, structured facts, deterministic extraction, evidence classification, party/time/unit matching, conflict detection, conditional applicability, partial drafting, exact citations, and answer fingerprints.
-- Every Form 202 and 207 item receives an internal requirement. Unrecognized narrative shapes remain explicitly unresolved rather than being guessed.
-- Current gross weekly income is separated from net, annual, historical, and wrong-party amounts.
-- Complaint allegations and unclassified notes are not promoted to supported facts.
+The supplied nine-page packet was first run unchanged: 3 fact observations and no answers or citations. The corrected HTTP upload/export run produced 116 observations, 23 populated fields, 71 validated citation references and 66 questions requiring evidence or review. DOB 05/30/1979 versus 05/03/1979 is a conflict with a blank answer. No defendant-row values were inserted into plaintiff responses. All 81 Form 202 items and all 55 Form 207 items remain in the exports.
 
-## Response lifecycle
+Source version, npm metadata and native bundle metadata identify 0.4.2 so a corrected process can be distinguished from 0.4.1. Native macOS/Windows builds, signing, notarization and a live browser walkthrough were not executed. Local HTTP, extraction, citation checks, PDF/Word exports and pytest were executed. See TEST_REPORT.md and verification materials for the exact scope and results.
 
-- `inquisitio/server.py` now stores `engine_status` separately from `workflow_status`.
-- Added explicit approval records, edit history, revalidation flags, and approval invalidation after edits or evidence reprocessing.
-- Added `POST /api/cases/{id}/evidence` for transactional PDF validation, append, and full deterministic reprocessing while preserving attorney edits.
-- Persisted structured facts privately in the case record while omitting the fact corpus from normal API responses.
-
-## Interface
-
-- `web/index.html` and `web/app.js` now collect responding/interrogating party context, show immutable engine findings, supported citations, missing information, conflicts, attorney workflow state, and explicit revalidate/approve controls.
-- Added evidence upload/reprocessing and a separate evidence-report download.
-- Updated `web/styles.css` for the new status, missing-information, conflict, and approval controls across desktop and mobile layouts.
-
-## Exports
-
-- Added an official-form-style PDF outcome with populated case caption, exact Form 202/207 questions and subparts, a response under every item, unresolved/review status, page numbering, and verification/certification sections.
-- Bundled DejaVu Sans under its included license for consistent cross-platform PDF typography.
-- `inquisitio/exporter.py` now marks response documents as attorney-review drafts, includes the full caption, engine/workflow status, and unresolved items, and retains all 81/55 original form items.
-- Added a separate traceability DOCX with question ID, proposed answer, exact quotation, PDF page, source ID, SHA-256 hash, missing information, conflicts, edit count, and approval state.
-
-## Tests and packaging
-
-- Added deterministic tests for party attribution, gross/net and current/historical distinctions, numerical and narrative drafts, corroboration, partial answers, conflicts, conditional applicability, allegations, exact citation linkage, all-item evaluation, approval, edit invalidation, malformed/scanned PDFs, reprocessing, and traceability exports.
-- Updated browser UAT expectations to exercise automatic drafting and explicit approval.
-- Bumped Python, npm, and macOS bundle metadata to 0.4.1.
-
-## Known limitations
-
-- Image-only PDFs are flagged for OCR; OCR is not performed.
-- The deterministic fact extractor intentionally covers explicit formulations and does not claim broad natural-language comprehension.
-- PDF export did not exist in the supplied baseline and is not added; the existing Word export is preserved and expanded.
-- Browser UAT and a native macOS build were not executable in the Linux delivery environment. See `TEST_REPORT.md`; this source package is not represented as a signed/notarized macOS GUI release.
+This release uses only the requested direct Python libraries and their installation dependencies. OCRmyPDF is pinned to the tested 16 series because its newer major release conflicts with the installed pdfplumber PDF-text dependency. Processing has no cloud or LLM calls.
